{
 "id": "2026-liu-pavilions",
 "name": "Pavilions in Chinese Garden Paintings: Free Viewing and Impressions",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the same five paintings the paper used: participants freely view five pavilion scenes from the 1744 album Forty Views of the Yuanmingyuan (Jiajing mingqin, Shangxia tianguang, Changchun xianguan, Xinghua chunguan, Tantan dangdang) in random order, 25 s each with a 5 s grey screen, exactly as in the paper, then answer its eight printed post-viewing questions: six 5-point agreement items (whole-to-detail viewing; noticing buildings before nature; pavilions as important in composition; pavilions drawing the gaze; imagining standing in the pavilion; pavilion position shaping spatial understanding), which painting made the deepest impression, and which elements become visual focal points. Ends with age, gender and prior exposure to Chinese landscape painting (the paper's five categories). Analysis: medians and agreement rates per item, choice shares. Original result (n = 30): pavilions were rarely the first thing fixated; 76.7% agreed they view whole-to-detail, 96.7% named buildings as focal elements, Xinghua chunguan made the deepest impression (36.7%). Differences from the original study: (1) no eye tracking, so the paper's fixation measures (time to first fixation, fixation share and count per area) are not collected; (2) the images are the Wikimedia Commons scans of the same paintings (four CC BY-SA 3.0, one public domain), with seals and some silk margin kept, not the authors' cropped files shown on a 55-inch 4K screen; (3) participants view on their own device, so size and distance vary; (4) the wording of the exposure question is mine.",
